# Reflection progression

A **reflection progression** is a sequence of theories in which each stage
extends the previous one by a statement the previous one could not decide
about itself. The name is the one used in the discussion behind this vault,
and the second half of the card, where the rule for choosing extensions is
itself revised, is the discussion's own proposal. The mathematical core is
established under other names: Turing's ordinal logics (1939) and Feferman's
transfinite recursive progressions of theories (1962).

## Description

### The thesis: put the human in

Neither of the two rigorous examples, the Gödel sentence and the
constructible universe, involves self-modification or feedback over time.
Both are static. The thesis that opened this part of the discussion was that
the missing ingredient is a person. A human can see that a theory leaves a
statement undecided, add axioms, and so expand the formalism, acting as a
thinking machine. On this view the human may be what makes the definition of
a strange loop essential and not merely formal.

### The progression

Written out, the proposal is

$$T_0\subseteq T_1\subseteq T_2\subseteq\cdots,\qquad T_{n+1}=T_n+\{G_n\},$$

where $G_n$ is the [[godel-sentence]] of $T_n$. Three facts qualify it.

- **Each step is outside the reach of the stage it extends.** $T_n$ does not
  prove $G_n$, so no deduction in $T_n$ yields $T_{n+1}$. The step is taken by
  someone who accepts $T_n$ and reflects on it. Seen from inside $T_n$ it is
  not deduction at all. See [[induction]].
- **It never ends in a complete theory.** Every stage that is still
  consistent, computably axiomatized and strong enough has a Gödel sentence of
  its own. The [[incompleteness-theorems]] apply again at each stage.
- **It is not automatically safe.** If $T_n$ proves only true arithmetical
  sentences, so does $T_{n+1}$. Consistency alone is not enough: there are
  consistent theories that prove their own inconsistency, and adding the
  Gödel sentence to one of those gives an inconsistent theory. The step is
  justified by trust in $T_n$, which is more than $T_n$ can express.

Turing and Feferman carried such sequences through the transfinite. Their
results limit what can be hoped for. Along suitable paths every true
arithmetical sentence becomes provable, but choosing the path requires
recognising which notations denote ordinals, and that is as hard as the
problem the progression was meant to solve. The incompleteness is moved, not
removed.

### The second loop: revising how to revise

The more interesting step in the discussion is to let the rule of extension
change as well.

$$(T_n,R_n)\longrightarrow(T_{n+1},R_{n+1})$$

Here $R_n$ encodes the current criteria for revising the theory: which
candidate axioms are admissible, and on what grounds.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
 A[Object theory T] --> B[Prove, test, inspect consequences]
 B --> C[Reflect on theory and its limits]
 C --> D[Human or agent selects revised axioms]
 D --> A
 C --> E[Reflect on criteria for revision]
 E --> D
```

The inner cycle revises the theory. The outer one revises the standards by
which the theory is revised. The plain progression has a fixed $R$: always
add the Gödel sentence.

### Static and evolving reflection

| | Static reflection | Evolving reflection |
|---|---|---|
| What happens | a theory expresses properties of its own syntax and proofs | reflective results change the rules or the criteria |
| Time | none | a sequence of stages |
| Vault card | [[reflective-formal-system]] | this one |
| Entry among the seven definitions | third and fourth | sixth, "evolving reflective" |

### The correction: the human is not required

The thesis was answered in two parts. A human is not a mathematical
requirement for a strange loop: a program can implement a theory progression,
and the definition of the progression does not mention who takes the step.
And what a human can do here that an algorithm cannot is a philosophical and
scientific question, not a corollary of Gödel's theorem.

What survives of the thesis is its structural half. The examples became
dynamic once something stood outside each stage and extended it. That role
can be filled by a person or by an agent. The cards [[human-in-the-loop]] and
[[reflective-agent]] take up the two cases.

### Why this matters for the vault

A vault that is evaluated, read and revised has the same two cycles. Cards
and cells are the theory. The conventions for accepting a revision are the
criteria. A revision that changes a card is the inner cycle. A revision that
changes how revisions are accepted is the outer one, and it is the outer one
that separates a reflective system from a [[feedback-loop]].

## Relations

- Each stage adds the [[godel-sentence]] {relation: uses} of the stage before.
- It never completes, by the [[incompleteness-theorems]] {relation: uses} applied at every stage.
- It is the evolving case, against the static [[reflective-formal-system]] {relation: contrasts}.
- Each extension is a non-deductive step of the kind described under [[induction]] {relation: uses}.
- Its inner cycle is [[revision]] {relation: example-of} of a theory in the light of [[reflection]] {relation: uses} on it.
- The step can be taken by a [[human-in-the-loop]] {relation: uses}, who is not mathematically required.
- The step can equally be taken by a [[reflective-agent]] {relation: uses}.
- It removes one instance of [[undecidability]] {relation: uses} at each stage and acquires another.
- It differs from a [[feedback-loop]] {relation: distinguished-from} by the outer cycle, in which the criteria of revision are revised.
- It is the sixth entry of [[definitions-of-strange-loop]] {relation: part-of}.
- It is the formal model for the cycle described in [[hmd-feedback-loop]] {relation: analogous-to}.
- It bears on the question [[building-a-loop-in-hmd]] {relation: answers}: the target is the outer cycle, not the inner one.

## Neighbouring concepts

- **[[godel-sentence]].** The sentence a theory cannot decide about its own
  provability. Here it is the material each new stage is made from.
- **[[reflective-formal-system]].** A theory that encodes its own syntax and
  proofs. It is one stage of a progression, considered alone.
- **[[human-in-the-loop]].** A person placed at the point where a result is
  judged and a change accepted. In a progression that point is the choice of
  the next axiom.
- **[[revision]].** Updating a model in the light of evidence. A progression
  is revision where the model is a theory and the evidence is the theory's
  own limit.
- **[[induction]].** Inference from observations to a hypothesis, always
  paired with deduction. Adopting an axiom is the step in mathematics that
  resembles it most.

